WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.028
Part one gave three rules, and a picture

2
00:00:06.104 --> 00:00:08.316
the determinant is the signed

3
00:00:08.393 --> 00:00:10.300
area spanned by the rows.

4
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.580
Leaning the shape never changes that area.

5
00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:17.723
Here is the matrix two, one, four, five.

6
00:00:17.723 --> 00:00:20.948
Its rows span this parallelogram

7
00:00:20.948 --> 00:00:22.370
with area six.

8
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.292
But how would you find the determinant of

9
00:00:25.355 --> 00:00:27.613
a one hundred by one hundred matrix?

10
00:00:27.613 --> 00:00:28.680
Pause and think.

11
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.707
Run elimination.

12
00:00:33.707 --> 00:00:37.415
Subtract two times row one from row two

13
00:00:37.415 --> 00:00:39.676
the parallelogram leans

14
00:00:39.676 --> 00:00:42.660
and row two becomes zero, three.

15
00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.717
Now the matrix is triangular

16
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.957
with the pivots two and

17
00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.103
three on its diagonal.

18
00:00:50.103 --> 00:00:54.303
One more lean, and the shape is a rectangle

19
00:00:54.303 --> 00:00:56.357
two wide, three high.

20
00:00:56.357 --> 00:00:57.290
Area six.

21
00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.020
That works for every size.

22
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.132
Elimination steps change nothing

23
00:01:03.132 --> 00:01:06.243
each row exchange flips the sign

24
00:01:06.243 --> 00:01:08.074
and you end with U.

25
00:01:08.165 --> 00:01:11.369
So the determinant is plus or minus

26
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.840
the product of the pivots.

27
00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.647
One more: three, one, three, five.

28
00:01:17.647 --> 00:01:20.800
Subtract row one from row two

29
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.054
and row two becomes zero, four.

30
00:01:24.054 --> 00:01:27.206
The pivots are three and four

31
00:01:27.206 --> 00:01:31.070
with no exchange: determinant twelve.

32
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.182
Now a matrix with a zero in

33
00:01:34.276 --> 00:01:38.309
the first pivot position: zero, two, three

34
00:01:38.309 --> 00:01:38.778
one.

35
00:01:38.778 --> 00:01:41.123
Its rows turn clockwise

36
00:01:41.123 --> 00:01:45.250
so elimination must start with an exchange.

37
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.164
After the swap the pivots are three and two.

38
00:01:50.164 --> 00:01:51.617
One exchange

39
00:01:51.617 --> 00:01:55.040
so the determinant is minus six.

40
00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:58.979
And if the rows are one, two and two, four

41
00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:01.566
the second is twice the first.

42
00:02:01.566 --> 00:02:03.819
The parallelogram is flat

43
00:02:03.819 --> 00:02:06.490
elimination runs out of pivots

44
00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.910
and the determinant is zero.

45
00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:11.334
That is consequence eight

46
00:02:11.413 --> 00:02:13.886
the determinant is zero exactly

47
00:02:13.965 --> 00:02:16.198
when the matrix is singular.

48
00:02:16.198 --> 00:02:19.229
NumPy computes determinants this way

49
00:02:19.229 --> 00:02:22.020
in about n cubed over three steps.

50
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.671
Two rules you will use all the time.

51
00:02:25.671 --> 00:02:26.268
First

52
00:02:26.268 --> 00:02:29.680
the transpose has the same determinant.

53
00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.116
Take two, one, zero, one.

54
00:02:32.116 --> 00:02:35.786
The parallelogram of its rows has area two.

55
00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.539
The parallelogram of its columns

56
00:02:38.622 --> 00:02:40.458
has a different shape

57
00:02:40.458 --> 00:02:42.710
but its area is two again.

58
00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.156
So every rule about rows

59
00:02:45.243 --> 00:02:47.252
also holds for columns

60
00:02:47.252 --> 00:02:49.785
two equal columns give zero

61
00:02:49.785 --> 00:02:53.540
and exchanging two columns flips the sign.

62
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.127
Second: a matrix multiplies every

63
00:02:57.218 --> 00:02:59.404
area by its determinant.

64
00:02:59.496 --> 00:03:02.593
So the determinant of a product is

65
00:03:02.684 --> 00:03:05.600
the product of the determinants.

66
00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:09.399
Start again from the unit square.

67
00:03:09.399 --> 00:03:13.684
Apply B, one, zero, one, one and a half

68
00:03:13.684 --> 00:03:17.238
the area grows by one and a half.

69
00:03:17.238 --> 00:03:20.896
Then apply A, two, one, zero, one

70
00:03:20.896 --> 00:03:22.150
it doubles.

71
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.010
Together, A B multiplies area by three.

72
00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.440
B A is a different matrix

73
00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.860
but its determinant is three as well.

74
00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:35.441
Undo it all, and the square comes back

75
00:03:35.441 --> 00:03:38.920
the inverse has one over the determinant.

76
00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.322
A rotation keeps every area

77
00:03:41.405 --> 00:03:44.636
so an orthogonal matrix has determinant

78
00:03:44.719 --> 00:03:46.210
plus or minus one.

79
00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:48.227
What not to do

80
00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:51.388
the determinant is not additive.

81
00:03:51.388 --> 00:03:53.208
Two I is I plus I

82
00:03:53.208 --> 00:03:55.986
yet its determinant is four

83
00:03:55.986 --> 00:03:57.710
not one plus one.

84
00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.717
And a second trap.

85
00:03:59.717 --> 00:04:02.754
Determinant zero means singular

86
00:04:02.846 --> 00:04:05.423
so surely a tiny determinant

87
00:04:05.515 --> 00:04:07.540
means nearly singular?

88
00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:08.387
No.

89
00:04:08.387 --> 00:04:12.389
Take a tenth of the fifty by fifty identity.

90
00:04:12.389 --> 00:04:15.858
Each of fifty rows shrinks by a tenth

91
00:04:15.947 --> 00:04:18.170
so the determinant is ten

92
00:04:18.259 --> 00:04:19.949
to the minus fifty.

93
00:04:19.949 --> 00:04:23.329
Yet it only shrinks the space evenly.

94
00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:26.531
Zoom back out, and nothing is lost

95
00:04:26.531 --> 00:04:29.110
its condition number is one

96
00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:30.800
the best possible.

97
00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.908
In the opposite direction: the shear one

98
00:04:34.908 --> 00:04:39.032
one thousand, zero, one has determinant one.

99
00:04:39.124 --> 00:04:42.240
Even a much milder shear stretches

100
00:04:42.331 --> 00:04:45.631
the square into a long, thin sliver.

101
00:04:45.723 --> 00:04:48.197
This one's condition number

102
00:04:48.289 --> 00:04:50.030
is about a million.

103
00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:52.655
So the size of the determinant

104
00:04:52.731 --> 00:04:53.792
is no warning.

105
00:04:53.792 --> 00:04:56.597
To test invertibility, count pivots

106
00:04:56.673 --> 00:04:58.720
or use the condition number

107
00:04:58.796 --> 00:05:00.540
of Lecture twenty-nine.

108
00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.547
So, to compute a determinant: eliminate

109
00:05:04.547 --> 00:05:06.688
count the row exchanges

110
00:05:06.688 --> 00:05:08.830
and multiply the pivots.

111
00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:11.400
Each exchange flips the sign.

112
00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:15.122
It is zero exactly for singular matrices

113
00:05:15.122 --> 00:05:16.685
and it multiplies

114
00:05:16.767 --> 00:05:19.646
the determinant of a product is the

115
00:05:19.728 --> 00:05:22.031
product of the determinants

116
00:05:22.031 --> 00:05:24.910
and the transpose changes nothing.

117
00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:26.631
Next lecture

118
00:05:26.631 --> 00:05:29.792
the formula behind the three rules

119
00:05:29.792 --> 00:05:32.865
and what the determinant measures

120
00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:34.709
an area with a sign

121
00:05:34.797 --> 00:05:37.606
and the factor by which a matrix

122
00:05:37.694 --> 00:05:39.450
scales every region.
